---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { marked } from "marked";
import markdown from "../../../content/ernest-hemingway.md?raw";

const html = marked.parse(markdown) as string;
const headings = [...markdown.matchAll(/^#{2,3}\s+(.+)$/gm)].map((match, index) => {
  const text = match[1].trim();
  const id = text.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, "-").replace(/^-|-$/g, "") + "-" + (index + 1);
  return { text, id, level: match[0].startsWith("###") ? 3 : 2 };
});
let headingIndex = 0;
const rendered = html.replace(/<h([23])>/g, (_match, level) => {
  const heading = headings[headingIndex++];
  return heading ? '<h' + level + ' id="' + heading.id + '">' : "<h" + level + ">";
});
---
<BaseLayout title="Meet Ernest Hemingway" description="Explore Ernest Hemingway's life, The Old Man and the Sea, his writing style, and practical American English.">
  <article class="section lesson-page" id="top">
    <div class="container">
      <a class="back-link" href="/american-english-method/learn/culture-arts/">← American Culture & Arts</a>
      <div class="lesson-meta">
        <span class="eyebrow">AMERICAN WRITERS</span>
        <span class="source-path">ERNEST HEMINGWAY · 1899–1961</span>
      </div>
      <figure class="hemingway-hero">
        <img
          src="https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/pnp/cph/3a00000/3a02000/3a02800/3a02860r.jpg"
          alt="Ernest Hemingway seated at a typewriter, photographed in 1939"
          loading="eager"
          fetchpriority="high"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Ernest Hemingway at a typewriter, 1939. Photograph by Lloyd R. Arnold.
          Library of Congress, Prints and Photographs Division.
          <a href="https://www.loc.gov/item/2002736785/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">View the original archive record</a>.
          Rights Advisory: No known restrictions on publication.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="hemingway-home">
        <img
          src="https://cdn.loc.gov/service/pnp/highsm/06200/06241v.jpg"
          alt="Ernest Hemingway's home in Havana, Cuba, photographed in 2010"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Finca Vigía, Hemingway's home in Havana, Cuba. Photograph by Carol M. Highsmith, 2010.
          Library of Congress, Prints and Photographs Division.
          <a href="https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2010638849/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">View the original archive record</a>.
          Rights Advisory: No known restrictions on publication.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <section class="hemingway-characters" aria-labelledby="characters-title">
        <h2 id="characters-title">Characters &amp; First-Eedition Visuals</h2>
        <p class="gallery-intro">
          These archival images offer historical visual references for some of Hemingway's best-known fictional worlds.
          They are not definitive portraits of the characters.
        </p>
        <div class="character-grid">
          <figure>
            <img
              src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/TheOldManGregorioFuentes.jpg"
              alt="Gregorio Fuentes, an elderly Cuban fisherman, photographed in 1998"
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <figcaption>
              <strong>Gregorio Fuentes — a real Cuban fisherman</strong>
              Photographed in 1998. Fuentes is sometimes described as a possible inspiration for Santiago in
              <em>The Old Man and the Sea</em>; he was a real person, not a portrait of the fictional character.
              Photograph by Toirelb. Released into the public domain.
              <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TheOldManGregorioFuentes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Image source &amp; rights</a>.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure>
            <img
              src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/48/Hemingway_farewell.png"
              alt="The 1929 first-edition cover of A Farewell to Arms, with an illustrated embracing couple"
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <figcaption>
              <strong>Frederic Henry &amp; Catherine Barkley — <em>A Farewell to Arms</em></strong>
              The first-edition dust jacket, designed by Cleo Damianakes, 1929. Its embracing couple offers a period
              visual interpretation of the novel's central relationship, rather than a literal character portrait.
              Public domain in the United States.
              <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hemingway_farewell.png" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Image source &amp; rights</a>.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure>
            <img
              src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7e/The_Sun_Also_Rises_dust_jacket.jpg"
              alt="The 1926 first-edition dust jacket of The Sun Also Rises, designed by Cleo Damianakes"
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <figcaption>
              <strong>Jake Barnes &amp; Lady Brett Ashley — <em>The Sun Also Rises</em></strong>
              The first-edition dust jacket, designed by Cleo Damianakes, 1926. The cover's stylised figure belongs
              to the visual world of the novel; it should not be treated as an authenticated portrait of Brett Ashley.
              Public domain in the United States.
              <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Sun_Also_Rises_dust_jacket.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Image source &amp; rights</a>.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p class="gallery-note">
          Rights note: public-domain status can differ by country. The two historic book jackets are identified as
          public domain in the United States; check local rules before reusing them elsewhere.
        </p>
      </section>

      <nav class="lesson-toc" aria-label="Page contents">
        <details>
          <summary>In this feature</summary>
          <ol>
            {headings.map((heading) => (
              <li class:list={{ "toc-subsection": heading.level === 3 }}>
                <a href={"#" + heading.id}>{heading.text}</a>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </details>
      </nav>
      <div class="markdown-content" set:html={rendered} />
      <div class="lesson-end"><a href="#top">Back to top ↑</a><a href="/american-english-method/learn/culture-arts/">← American Culture & Arts</a></div>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
<style>
.hemingway-hero,.hemingway-home{max-width:760px;margin:2rem auto 2.5rem}
.hemingway-hero img{display:block;width:100%;max-width:150px;height:auto;margin:0 auto;object-fit:contain;background:#f4f1e9;border-radius:6px}
.hemingway-home img{display:block;width:100%;max-height:460px;object-fit:cover;object-position:center;border-radius:8px}
.hemingway-hero figcaption,.hemingway-home figcaption{margin:.65rem auto 0;max-width:650px;color:#6b7280;font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.55;text-align:center}
.hemingway-hero figcaption a,.hemingway-home figcaption a{color:#8b6b32;text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:3px}
.hemingway-characters{margin:3.5rem auto;max-width:1050px}
.hemingway-characters h2{text-align:center;margin-bottom:.6rem}
.gallery-intro,.gallery-note{max-width:760px;margin:0 auto 1.5rem;text-align:center;color:#6b7280;line-height:1.65}
.character-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr));gap:1.35rem;align-items:start}
.character-grid figure{margin:0;min-width:0}
.character-grid img{display:block;width:100%;height:280px;object-fit:contain;background:#f4f1e9;border-radius:7px}
.character-grid figcaption{padding:.75rem .15rem 0;color:#6b7280;font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.6}
.character-grid figcaption strong{display:block;color:var(--color-text, #273244);margin-bottom:.35rem}
.character-grid figcaption a{display:inline-block;margin-top:.35rem;color:#8b6b32;text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:3px}
.gallery-note{font-size:.8rem;margin-top:1.25rem}
@media(max-width:760px){.character-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:1.8rem}.character-grid img{height:auto;max-height:360px}.character-grid figure{max-width:560px;margin:0 auto;width:100%}}
</style>
